Eventually, vide letter of Termination of Employment dated 26/07/2018 signed by COW-1, Ang Ting Cheong (page 23 of CLB) the Company decided to terminate the Claimant’s employment in accordance to Clause 10 in the Employment Contract signed by the Claimant on 28/04/2014. This letter of termination is to inform the Claimant that her employment with the Company will end as of 06/08/2018, as requested by the Claimant in her email. According to the said Employment Contract, the Company paid her two (2) months’ salary in lieu of notice and her balance of annual leave. The termination of the Claimant’s employment is due to the following reasons: - (i) Company has been making financial losses for past years;
(ii) As part of Company’s restructuring efforts, to consolidate functions of Finance and Purchasing over the years for improving productivity and cost down whereby the Company had sold off the hatchery effective 01/08/2018; and
(iii) With streamline of workflow, the Company consolidated the Human Resources function as well.

Be that as it may, the Claimant had admitted: -
a. The Claimant had admitted during his cross examination that he had never stated that the contents of the show cause letter at page 20 COB were incorrect;
b. The Claimant had admitted during his cross examination that he had never stated in his reply letter at page 22 COB that the contents of the show cause letter at page 20 COB were inaccurate/incorrect;
c. The Claimant had admitted during his cross examination that he had never stated in his reply letter at page 22 COB that he was not guilty of the charges;
d. The Claimant had admitted that he had attended the domestic inquiry and defense himself; e. The Claimant had admitted during his cross examination that he understood the charges read to him during the domestic inquiry;
f. The Claimant had admitted during his cross examination that it was not within his job scope and/or duties as Crew to recommend contractor and plumber and/or to introduce contractor and plumber as what he did on this case;
g. The Claimant had admitted during his cross examination that the water meter installation can only be done by contractor or plumber who have license with the Company and he knew that CLW-2 and Nordin have no license with the Company; and
h. The Claimant had admitted during his cross examination that the impact of the police report made by COW- 1 had tarnished the Company's image.

The reference raises the primary issue of whether the Claimant’s dismissal was with “just cause or excuse” under Section 20 IRA 1967. Concretely, the Court must determine if the Company established each of the alleged misconduct charges and, if so, whether the misconduct justified summary dismissal. Related issues include:
(a) Whether the Claimant committed the charged acts (Mr. Azman appointment, banking arrangement, etc.) as alleged;
(b) whether those acts breached lawful and reasonable orders or essential terms of employment;
(c) whether the dismissal was proportionate to any proven wrongdoing;
(d) the credibility of the parties’ evidence. The Claimant also raised a “legitimate expectation” argument that he would be made DG, but this Court must consider it in light of his contractual terms. The parties did not dispute that the onus lies on the employer to prove just cause, and that proof is on the balance of probabilities.

The Honourable Court’s task on Section 20 of IRA 1967 reference is “two-fold”:
(a) to determine whether the alleged misconduct is proved, and if so whether that misconduct constitutes “just cause or excuse” for dismissal. Under Goon Kwee Phoy V. J & P Coats (M) Bhd [1981]2 MLJ 129 (Federal Court), if the employer’s reason is given, the Court must inquire whether that reason is made out;
(b) if it is not proved on the balance of probabilities, the dismissal must be held without just cause. The employer bears the burden of proof, and the standard of proof is the civil standard (preponderance of probabilities). By Section 30(5) IRA the Industrial Court must act “according to equity and good conscience”, focusing on substantial merits rather than technicalities.

Learned counsel for the claimant also cited the Federal Court’s case of Leong Hoong Khie v PP & Another Case [1986] CLJ Rep 179, whereby the apex court held that the general rule is that hearsay evidence is not admissible as proof of fact which has been stated by a third party who cannot be cross-examined and the danger that the evidence may be concocted, fabricated and tailored to suit the witness’s testimony. The learned counsel also quoted: a. Grace Yong Yee Fui & Anor v. Hock Sheng Resources Sdn Bhd [2025] 3 ILR 579;
b. Josita Ket Kang En@Karlen v Golden Horse Palace Berhad [2025] lLRU 0300;
c. Chong Chee Meng v. Multiple Pipeline Services Sdn Bhd [2025] 2 lLR 563; and
d. Abdul Hadee Ahmad v Tenaga National Berhad [2025] lLRU 0891,
to show that the Industrial Courts applied the principle propounded in Leong Hoong Khie, supra.

The Learned Chairman in the case of Harris Solid State (M) Sdn. Bhd., Harris Advanced Technology (M) Sdn. Bhd., Har- Ris Semiconductor (M) Sdn. Bhd. v. Bruno Gentil S/O Pereira & 18 Ors., Kesatuan Pekerja-Pekerja Harris Solid State (M) Sdn. Bhd. [1992] 2 ILR 316 held: “The learned counsel for the companies was at pain in his presentation to the Court, to dress up a paraphernalia of technicalities and legalities, though impressive it may be, which is groundless. The case must not be entrammelled with technicalities and legalities. It is a settled principle that the primary goal in settling an industrial dispute in this Court is social justice, vide, obiter dictum of Raja Azlan Shah CJ (as he then was) in Non-Metallic Products Manufacturing Employees' Union & Ors. v. South East Asia Firebricks Sdn. Bhd. [1976] 2 MLJ, and Changla CJ in Parakash Confectionery Mills Ltd. v. State of Bombay [1957] 1 LLJ. This is reinforced by the spirit and intendment of the Industrial Relations Act 1967, s. 30(5) which states that;
The Court shall act according to equity, good conscience and substantial merits of the case without regard to technicalities and legal form.
One wonders why should a sinuous web of technicalities be wound around the issue, and thereby one lost the wood for the trees. Mindful of the fact that this is a Court of equity and good conscience, the approach much be as realistic as possible. A ruling to the contrary would be illusory, unrealistic and astigmatic.” (emphasis added)

Clause 41 and 42 of the Code is a provision on Procedure for Disciplinary Action as follows :
“ 41. An employer should ensure that a fair, effective and expeditious procedure exists for dealing with disciplinary matters. Such procedure may be established in consultation with the employees representatives or trade union as appropriate. The employer should define and make known to each employee the rules of work and the disciplinary action which may follow if they are broken. Penalties should be graduated according to the seriousness of the offence. An employee should not, except in cases of gross misconduct, be dismissed for a first offence.
42. The disciplinary procedure should be in writing and be made known to each employee. The proceedings should be conducted in accordance with the rules of natural justice and should “ a) provide for the employee to be informed, in writing, of the misconduct;
b) specify who has the authority to take what forms of disciplinary action;
c) provide for full and speedy consideration by the employer of all the relevant facts;
d) give the employee the opportunity to state his case and the right to be represented by his workers’ representative or trade union official
e) in the case of less serious offences, provide, in the first instance, for a warning by the employee’s immediate superior;
f) in the case of more serious offences, provide for a formal warning in writing, setting out the circumstances and the disciplinary action to which an employee will be liable if he commits a further offence, and require a copy if this record to be given to the employee and if he so wishes to his employees’ representative or trade union official;
g) provide for a right of appeal against disciplinary action to a higher level of management not previously involved.
